Logging Best Practices – Writing Useful Production Logs 📜

🔹 Why Do Logging Best Practices Matter?
Good logs make troubleshooting faster. Poor logs create noise and make important errors difficult to find.
👉 The goal is quality logs, not more logs.

🔇 Avoid Log Noise
Too many unnecessary logs can cause:
❌ Difficult searching
 ❌ Higher storage costs 💰
 ❌ Slower log analysis
 ❌ Important errors getting buried
Avoid logging the same information repeatedly unless it provides real value.

🎯 Write Useful Logs
A useful log should provide enough context to understand an event.
Include:
✔ Timestamp ⏱️
 ✔ Log level
 ✔ Service name
 ✔ Error/message
 ✔ Request or correlation ID
 ✔ Relevant context
Example:
ERROR payment-api
request_id=abc123
Database connection timeout

This is much more useful than:
ERROR: Failed

💡 Real-world Example
An API starts returning errors 🚨
Poor log:
ERROR: Something went wrong

❌ No useful context.
Better log:
ERROR: payment-api
request_id=abc123
Database connection timeout
✅ Engineers can quickly investigate the issue.

🧠 Key Takeaways
✔ Avoid unnecessary logs 🔇
 ✔ Log meaningful information 🎯
 ✔ Include enough context 🔍
 ✔ Use structured logs when possible 📦
 ✔ Good logs = Faster debugging 🚀
